{"module":"surveillance","voice":"female","duration":190.5,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Watched: surveillance of Black and Brown communities","start":0.0,"end":3.33,"words":[[0.06,0.5],[0.76,1.41],[1.41,1.56],[1.56,1.85],[1.85,2.07],[2.07,2.44],[2.44,3.17]]},{"el":"intro","text":"A weaponized 911 call is one person watching another and calling the police.","start":4.13,"end":9.62,"words":[[4.19,4.33],[4.33,4.96],[4.96,6.3],[6.3,6.51],[6.51,6.65],[6.65,6.86],[6.86,7.21],[7.21,7.63],[7.63,7.98],[7.98,8.2],[8.2,8.55],[8.55,8.69],[8.69,9.11]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Governments have done the same thing on a much larger scale.","start":9.97,"end":13.11,"words":[[10.02,10.63],[10.63,10.82],[10.82,11.0],[11.0,11.12],[11.12,11.37],[11.37,11.55],[11.55,11.67],[11.67,11.79],[11.79,12.04],[12.04,12.34],[12.34,12.71]]},{"el":"h0","text":"COINTELPRO","start":13.46,"end":15.08,"words":[[13.46,14.9]]},{"el":"s0","text":"From 1956 to 1971, the FBI ran a secret program to “expose, disrupt, misdirect, discredit, or otherwise neutralize” political groups, with a heavy focus on civil rights and Black organizations.","start":15.78,"end":32.68,"words":[[15.78,16.1],[16.1,17.35],[17.35,17.51],[17.51,18.92],[19.19,19.35],[19.35,19.9],[19.9,20.13],[20.13,20.29],[20.29,20.76],[20.76,21.38],[21.38,21.54],[21.54,22.17],[22.44,23.07],[23.34,24.2],[24.48,25.26],[25.54,25.61],[25.61,26.16],[26.16,26.95],[26.95,27.65],[27.65,28.12],[28.39,28.63],[28.63,28.79],[28.79,29.1],[29.1,29.57],[29.57,29.73],[29.73,30.12],[30.12,30.51],[30.51,30.74],[30.74,31.06],[31.06,32.15]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Wiretaps on Dr. King were approved in 1963, and in 1964 the FBI mailed him an anonymous letter that urged him to kill himself.","start":33.03,"end":43.12,"words":[[33.08,33.67],[33.67,33.82],[33.82,34.26],[34.63,34.85],[34.85,35.0],[35.0,35.44],[35.44,35.59],[35.59,36.85],[37.11,37.33],[37.33,37.48],[37.48,38.59],[38.59,38.74],[38.74,39.25],[39.25,39.62],[39.62,39.85],[39.85,39.99],[39.99,40.59],[40.59,40.88],[40.88,41.1],[41.1,41.4],[41.4,41.62],[41.62,41.77],[41.77,41.99],[41.99,42.58]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The program was exposed in 1971 when activists broke into an FBI office in Media, Pennsylvania, and mailed the files to reporters.","start":43.47,"end":52.82,"words":[[43.53,43.66],[43.66,44.21],[44.21,44.41],[44.41,44.96],[44.96,45.1],[45.1,46.26],[46.26,46.47],[46.47,47.08],[47.08,47.42],[47.42,47.7],[47.7,47.84],[47.84,48.31],[48.31,48.59],[48.59,48.72],[48.72,49.2],[49.44,50.33],[50.57,50.78],[50.78,51.12],[51.12,51.26],[51.26,51.6],[51.6,51.74],[51.74,52.28]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The Senate's Church Committee investigated in 1975–76.","start":53.17,"end":58.21,"words":[[53.22,53.36],[53.36,53.77],[53.77,54.11],[54.11,54.52],[54.52,55.4],[55.4,55.54],[55.54,57.71]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Fred Hampton","start":59.11,"end":60.19,"words":[[59.11,59.47],[59.47,60.1]]},{"el":"s1","text":"On December 4, 1969, Chicago police, working with information from an FBI informant inside the Black Panther Party, raided an apartment before dawn and killed Illinois Panther leader Fred Hampton, 21, in his bed, along with Mark Clark.","start":60.89,"end":78.83,"words":[[60.93,61.09],[61.09,61.64],[61.64,61.88],[62.16,63.59],[63.86,64.42],[64.42,64.89],[65.17,65.57],[65.57,65.8],[65.8,66.6],[66.6,66.91],[66.91,67.07],[67.07,67.63],[67.63,68.26],[68.26,68.74],[68.74,68.89],[68.89,69.21],[69.21,69.61],[69.61,70.0],[70.28,70.76],[70.76,70.92],[70.92,71.55],[71.55,71.87],[71.87,72.1],[72.1,72.34],[72.34,72.66],[72.66,73.13],[73.13,73.53],[73.53,73.85],[73.85,74.16],[74.16,74.8],[75.08,75.87],[76.15,76.3],[76.3,76.54],[76.54,76.86],[77.14,77.45],[77.45,77.69],[77.69,77.93],[77.93,78.32]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Nearly all the shots came from police.","start":79.18,"end":82.22,"words":[[79.23,79.68],[79.68,79.86],[79.86,80.03],[80.03,80.39],[80.39,80.75],[80.75,81.11],[81.11,81.64]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In 1982 the city, county and federal government paid $1.85 million to settle the survivors' lawsuit.","start":82.57,"end":91.65,"words":[[82.62,82.78],[82.78,83.85],[83.85,84.02],[84.02,84.43],[84.71,85.2],[85.2,85.45],[85.45,86.03],[86.03,86.76],[86.76,87.09],[87.09,88.65],[88.65,89.14],[89.14,89.31],[89.31,89.72],[89.72,89.88],[89.88,90.54],[90.54,91.03]]},{"el":"h2","text":"After 9/11 and today","start":92.55,"end":95.25,"words":[[92.6,92.99],[92.99,94.36],[94.36,94.65],[94.65,95.14]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The NYPD's “Demographics Unit” mapped and monitored Muslim neighborhoods, mosques and businesses for years; the department later testified it never produced a single terrorism lead, and it was disbanded in 2014.","start":95.95,"end":110.23,"words":[[95.99,96.15],[96.15,96.9],[96.9,97.73],[97.73,98.11],[98.11,98.41],[98.41,98.63],[98.63,99.16],[99.16,99.61],[99.61,100.37],[100.63,101.01],[101.01,101.24],[101.24,101.84],[101.84,101.99],[101.99,102.37],[102.63,102.78],[102.78,103.46],[103.46,103.84],[103.84,104.52],[104.52,104.67],[104.67,104.97],[104.97,105.57],[105.57,105.72],[105.72,106.17],[106.17,106.85],[106.85,107.15],[107.42,107.64],[107.64,107.8],[107.8,108.02],[108.02,108.7],[108.7,108.85],[108.85,109.76]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2017 an FBI report invented the category “Black identity extremists.”","start":110.58,"end":116.25,"words":[[110.63,110.78],[110.78,111.77],[111.77,111.91],[111.91,112.41],[112.41,112.76],[112.76,113.33],[113.33,113.47],[113.47,114.04],[114.04,114.32],[114.32,114.96],[114.96,115.81]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Facial recognition has led to wrongful arrests of Black people, including Robert Williams (2020) and Porcha Woodruff, who was eight months pregnant (2023); a 2019 federal test (NIST) found many algorithms produced more false matches for Black and Asian faces.","start":116.6,"end":136.2,"words":[[116.61,117.09],[117.09,117.88],[117.88,118.12],[118.12,118.35],[118.35,118.51],[118.51,118.99],[118.99,119.46],[119.46,119.62],[119.62,119.94],[119.94,120.41],[120.69,121.33],[121.33,121.72],[121.72,122.28],[122.28,123.23],[123.51,123.74],[123.74,124.14],[124.14,124.69],[124.97,125.13],[125.13,125.37],[125.37,125.61],[125.61,126.0],[126.0,126.64],[126.64,127.9],[128.18,128.34],[128.34,129.37],[129.37,129.92],[129.92,130.24],[130.24,130.56],[130.84,131.23],[131.23,131.55],[131.55,132.34],[132.34,132.98],[132.98,133.13],[133.13,133.45],[133.45,133.93],[133.93,134.08],[134.08,134.4],[134.4,134.64],[134.64,135.04],[135.04,135.59]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Does predictive policing work?","start":137.1,"end":139.06,"words":[[137.15,137.34],[137.34,137.91],[137.91,138.35],[138.35,138.6]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Some departments use software to predict where crime will happen or who will commit it.","start":139.76,"end":144.49,"words":[[139.81,140.01],[140.01,140.68],[140.68,140.88],[140.88,141.35],[141.35,141.49],[141.49,141.95],[141.95,142.16],[142.16,142.49],[142.49,142.69],[142.69,143.03],[143.03,143.09],[143.09,143.23],[143.23,143.43],[143.43,143.76],[143.76,143.97]]},{"el":"s3","text":"A RAND evaluation of a Shreveport program found no statistically significant effect on crime, and critics say such systems recycle biased arrest data into more stops in the same neighborhoods.","start":144.84,"end":157.83,"words":[[144.89,145.04],[145.04,145.35],[145.35,146.21],[146.21,146.36],[146.36,146.52],[146.52,147.06],[147.06,147.68],[147.68,148.07],[148.07,148.31],[148.31,149.16],[149.16,150.02],[150.02,150.41],[150.41,150.56],[150.56,151.03],[151.3,151.53],[151.53,152.08],[152.08,152.31],[152.31,152.62],[152.62,153.17],[153.17,153.79],[153.79,154.26],[154.26,154.64],[154.64,155.03],[155.03,155.34],[155.34,155.5],[155.5,155.89],[155.89,156.04],[156.04,156.2],[156.2,156.51],[156.51,157.29]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Supporters say better-designed systems can help.","start":158.18,"end":160.98,"words":[[158.21,158.66],[158.66,158.85],[158.85,159.57],[159.57,160.02],[160.02,160.21],[160.21,160.54]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The evidence so far is thin.","start":161.33,"end":163.67,"words":[[161.38,161.55],[161.55,162.17],[162.17,162.43],[162.43,162.61],[162.61,162.79],[162.79,163.14]]},{"el":"h4","text":"The neighborhood app","start":164.57,"end":165.71,"words":[[164.62,164.79],[164.79,165.45],[165.45,165.62]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Neighborhood apps and doorbell cameras have made it easy to report anyone as “suspicious.”","start":166.41,"end":171.35,"words":[[166.46,167.03],[167.03,167.24],[167.24,167.45],[167.45,167.81],[167.81,168.23],[168.23,168.44],[168.44,168.73],[168.73,168.87],[168.87,169.08],[169.08,169.22],[169.22,169.58],[169.58,170.0],[170.0,170.14],[170.14,170.78]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2016, after complaints that its crime and safety posts were full of racial profiling, Nextdoor changed its reporting form to require details beyond race.","start":171.7,"end":183.43,"words":[[171.75,171.91],[171.91,173.0],[173.27,173.59],[173.59,174.36],[174.36,174.6],[174.6,174.83],[174.83,175.22],[175.22,175.46],[175.46,175.92],[175.92,176.39],[176.39,176.55],[176.55,176.78],[176.78,176.94],[176.94,177.4],[177.4,178.26],[178.53,179.08],[179.08,179.7],[179.7,179.94],[179.94,180.48],[180.48,180.71],[180.71,180.87],[180.87,181.42],[181.42,181.96],[181.96,182.43],[182.43,182.82]]},{"el":"s4","text":"That is the same principle as the CAREN Act: being a person of color in a place is not suspicious.","start":183.78,"end":189.56,"words":[[183.83,184.03],[184.03,184.17],[184.17,184.3],[184.3,184.57],[184.57,185.18],[185.18,185.31],[185.31,185.45],[185.45,185.79],[185.79,186.06],[186.29,186.63],[186.63,186.77],[186.77,187.1],[187.1,187.24],[187.24,187.51],[187.51,187.64],[187.64,187.78],[187.78,188.12],[188.12,188.25],[188.25,188.45],[188.45,189.06]]}]}